# Testing And CI

- Use Vitest for package tests and place tests next to the owning package under `packages/*/tests`.
- Test behavior visible to users or package consumers, not private implementation details.
- Add or update tests when changing validation, rendering, AI guardrails, CLI parsing, template loading, or public exports.

## Test Integrity

- Tests must describe the expected behavior of the product or public API, not the current implementation.
- Do not weaken, delete, skip, or rewrite assertions only to make a failing test pass.
- If a test fails, first assume it may be exposing a real bug or mismatch in behavior. Investigate the implementation before changing the test.
- Update tests only when the intended behavior or public contract has intentionally changed.
- When changing an existing test, explain why the previous expectation was wrong or obsolete.
- Do not mock away the behavior being tested. Mocks may isolate external dependencies, but must not hide the core failure.
- Avoid broad snapshots or overly generic assertions that can pass while behavior is broken.
- For bug fixes, prefer adding or keeping a regression test that fails before the fix and passes after it.

## Verification

- Before finishing substantive TypeScript changes, run `pnpm typecheck` and `pnpm test` from the repo root when practical.
- Run `pnpm build` when changing package exports, tsup config, package manifests, or code that may affect generated declarations.
- GitHub Actions should call the same package manager and scripts used locally. Keep workflow logic minimal and avoid duplicating build behavior in YAML.
- If a verification command cannot be run, report that explicitly with the reason.

